{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfSRcAwXSjjbeypUwnsPU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Vcdw5hhHGSPqhWubcv8qW7","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"NASA Voyager 2 Uranus \"Light conditions were\" \"times less than terrestrial conditions\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12140,"cache_read_input_tokens":1482,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12140},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aba18ead-bc2e-4b2f-a09e-cadd0525d730","uuid":"825b7600-cc58-42d9-8662-54c3f9773ec1","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:13:05.515Z","request_id":"req_011CfSRcAVVBksfZfnVxcW6A","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Vcdw5hhHGSPqhWubcv8qW7":{"query":"NASA Voyager 2 Uranus \"Light conditions were\" \"times less than terrestrial conditions\""}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vcdw5hhHGSPqhWubcv8qW7","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"NASA Voyager 2 Uranus \"Light conditions were\" \"times less than terrestrial conditions\"\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants — NASA Science\",\"url\":\"<redacted> at Uranus - NASA Science\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Uranus with Voyager 2 | The Planetary Society\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 38-Year-Old Voyager 2 Data Finally Solves Uranus’s Perplexing Mysteries\",\"url\":\"<redacted> as seen by NASA's Voyager 2 - NASA Science\",\"url\":\"<redacted> to the Uranus Data Archive Page\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Searches\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Searches\",\"url\":\"<redacted> found the information you were looking for! \"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions\" during NASA's Voyager 2 mission to Uranus. This statement comes from the official NASA Science website about Voyager 2's encounter with Uranus, which occurred when the spacecraft began observing the planet on November 4, 1985, with its closest approach on January 24, 1986.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aba18ead-bc2e-4b2f-a09e-cadd0525d730","uuid":"36fdbd66-cc36-4301-90ed-48ddeb59ce1f","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:13:08.898Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"NASA Voyager 2 Uranus \"Light conditions were\" \"times less than terrestrial conditions\"","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_0192Abp7u52YxzqtKve4g3CF","content":[{"title":"Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants — NASA Science","url":"<redacted> at Uranus - NASA Science","url":"<redacted> Uranus with Voyager 2 | The Planetary Society","url":"<redacted> 38-Year-Old Voyager 2 Data Finally Solves Uranus’s Perplexing Mysteries","url":"<redacted> as seen by NASA's Voyager 2 - NASA Science","url":"<redacted> to the Uranus Data Archive Page","url":"<redacted> Searches","url":"<redacted> Searches","url":"<redacted> found the information you were looking for! \"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions\" during NASA's Voyager 2 mission to Uranus. This statement comes from the official NASA Science website about Voyager 2's encounter with Uranus, which occurred when the spacecraft began observing the planet on November 4, 1985, with its closest approach on January 24, 1986."],"durationSeconds":3.375276584000001,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfSRd3CyAXHKck2D7G2Aa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F3C1FszqaLonyWksk67H2s","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"<redacted> verbatim the entire \"Uranus\" section of the mission description (from \"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled\" through the Challenger accident sentence)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1755,"cache_read_input_tokens":13622,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1755},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aba18ead-bc2e-4b2f-a09e-cadd0525d730","uuid":"e60ba46a-6de1-427a-91ca-7dd6866745a8","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:13:11.453Z","request_id":"req_011CfSRd2q9Koa5gfAQsUYrr","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01F3C1FszqaLonyWksk67H2s":{"url":"<redacted> verbatim the entire \"Uranus\" section of the mission description (from \"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled\" through the Challenger accident sentence)."}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F3C1FszqaLonyWksk67H2s","type":"tool_result","content":"# Voyager 2 at Uranus Section\n\nHere's the verbatim text from the \"Voyager 2 at Uranus\" section:\n\n\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\n\nIn fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible.\n\nThe Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\n\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\n\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the \"older\" nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\n\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and opacity.\n\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus' larger moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\n\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aba18ead-bc2e-4b2f-a09e-cadd0525d730","uuid":"b46f1742-3233-4e1d-ab68-1a5784b7ff26","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:13:21.084Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":327590,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Voyager 2 at Uranus Section\n\nHere's the verbatim text from the \"Voyager 2 at Uranus\" section:\n\n\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\n\nIn fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible.\n\nThe Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\n\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\n\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the \"older\" nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\n\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and opacity.\n\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus' larger moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\n\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\"","durationMs":9626,"url":"<redacted>
