{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfSRdwLXfqykgACoXfdyi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```json\n{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n      \"reason\": \"Both titles are identical; the base document's title is kept.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\"segment\": \"b1\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"reason\": \"Title: identical base title kept, stated once.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a2\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\", \"reason\": \"Spacecraft, encounter count and duration corrected against NASA page.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a4\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\", \"reason\": \"Typo fixed; next target, craft and study time corrected per NASA.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a5\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\", \"reason\": \"Craft, observation type, start date and signal time corrected per NASA.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a6\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\", \"reason\": \"Light-level factor corrected per NASA page.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a7\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\", \"reason\": \"Time zone, year and swapped distance units corrected per NASA.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b2\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\", \"reason\": \"Moon count, names, author, year, rings and tilt corrected per NASA.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b3\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\", \"reason\": \"Wind units, ocean wording and depth figures corrected per NASA.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b5\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\", \"reason\": \"Moon names and 'larger' corrected per NASA page.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b6\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\", \"reason\": \"Thousands separators and 'decade-long' corrected per NASA page.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b9\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\", \"reason\": \"Timing, death toll and launch date corrected per NASA page.\"}\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\",\n  \"additions\": [\n    {\"statement\": \"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\", \"corrects\": \"Although Voyager 3 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\", \"basis\": \"citation\", \"source\": \"NASA Science, 'Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants', <redacted> read during this merge\", \"reason\": \"NASA's page says Voyager 2, two encounters, about 4.5 years.\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\", \"corrects\": \"The Ur anus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\", \"basis\": \"citation\", \"source\": \"NASA Science, 'Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants', <redacted> read during this merge\", \"reason\": \"NASA page gives Neptune, Voyager 2 and 5.5 hours.\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\", \"corrects\": \"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\", \"basis\": \"citation\", \"source\": \"NASA Science, 'Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants', <redacted> read during this merge\", \"reason\": \"NASA page gives long-range, Nov. 4, 1985 and 2.5 hours.\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\", \"corrects\": \"Light conditions were five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions.\", \"basis\": \"citation\", \"source\": \"NASA Science, 'Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants', <redacted> read during this merge\", \"reason\": \"NASA page states 400 times less.\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\", \"corrects\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles).\", \"basis\": \"citation\", \"source\": \"NASA Science, 'Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants', <redacted> read during this merge\", \"reason\": \"NASA page gives 17:59 UT, 1986, 50,640 miles (81,500 km).\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\", \"corrects\": \"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\", \"basis\": \"citation\", \"source\": \"NASA Science, 'Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants', <redacted> read during this merge\", \"reason\": \"NASA page lists 10 moons, Shakespeare, 1787, two/nine rings, 55°.\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\", \"corrects\": \"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\", \"basis\": \"citation\", \"source\": \"NASA Science, 'Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants', <redacted> read during this merge\", \"reason\": \"NASA page gives 450 mph (724 km/h), ocean, 497 miles (800 km).\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\", \"corrects\": \"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\", \"basis\": \"citation\", \"source\": \"NASA Science, 'Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants', <redacted> read during this merge\", \"reason\": \"NASA page names Ariel, Umbriel, Titania as larger moons.\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\", \"corrects\": \"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels.\", \"basis\": \"citation\", \"source\": \"NASA Science, 'Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants', <redacted> read during this merge\", \"reason\": \"NASA page gives 17,560 miles (28,260 km), decade-long.\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\", \"corrects\": \"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986.\", \"basis\": \"citation\", \"source\": \"NASA Science, 'Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants', <redacted> read during this merge\", \"reason\": \"NASA page: same week, seven astronauts, Jan. 28, 1986.\"}\n  ]\n}\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1018,"cache_read_input_tokens":15377,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1018},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aba18ead-bc2e-4b2f-a09e-cadd0525d730","uuid":"eefdfd0e-4d8c-4e96-9092-4bb1099056b1","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:14:14.224Z","request_id":"req_011CfSRdvuj8brpZQTzt9nUk"}
{"duration_api_ms":76071,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"aba18ead-bc2e-4b2f-a09e-cadd0525d730","total_cost_usd":0.3350032,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14913,"cache_read_input_tokens":30481,"output_tokens":8429,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3327},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14913,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7332,"cache_read_input_tokens":15377,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1018,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1018},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":6,"outputTokens":8429,"cacheReadInputTokens":30481,"cacheCreationInputTokens":14913,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.29400420000000005,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":3327,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":27184,"outputTokens":763,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":1,"costUSD":0.040998999999999994,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same week by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n      \"reason\": \"Both titles are identical; the base document's title is kept.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\"segment\": \"b1\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"reason\": \"Title: identical base title kept, stated once.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a2\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\", \"reason\": \"Spacecraft, encounter count and duration corrected against NASA page.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a4\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\", \"reason\": \"Typo fixed; next target, craft and study time corrected per NASA.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a5\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's long-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\", \"reason\": \"Craft, observation type, start date and signal time corrected per NASA.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a6\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\", \"reason\": \"Light-level factor corrected per NASA page.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a7\", \